Author: frank <hellmann@pik-potsdam.de>
Date:   Mon Nov 23 00:58:43 2026 +0100

    runtime: a match at a kind, or its absence, holds only while the set's entries for type values do
    
    A kind of type object (`DataType`, `UnionAll`, `Union`, `Type{T}` with a free `T`) meets a set where the set takes
    a type value (compiler §4.10), and that rests on the set's entries for type values, which are kept on each value's
    TypeName, not on any type of the query. The worlds of a lookup were narrowed only by the entries reachable from the
    query's types, so a lookup at `Tuple{typeof(g), Type}` asked in a world before `Type{S}`'s membership in `X` was
    valid in the worlds where it holds, and the method cache's entry at that widened signature, made from a lookup at
    `Type{S}` in the earlier world, answered `g(::Any)` there; code compiled at the kind after the membership was taken
    back ran in the worlds where it held (E-542's pattern 8). Now a range asked at a query with a kind at a covariant
    place is narrowed by the entries for type values of the signature's sets and of the sets that join them
    (`jl_type_has_kind`, `value_entries_range`), and the typemap's visitor asks it also where the intersection is empty.
    
    Reference: compiler §4.10, §5.3 (a match's worlds through an entry are the entry's).
    Test: experiments/implementation/w171/gates/n42_kind_value_worlds.jl.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

